sec-2-3-1","identifier":"sec-2-3-1","html_id":"sec-2-3-1","key":"A16fWykuW4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many useful algorithms are recursive in structure: to solve a given problem, they\nrecurse (call themselves) one or more times to handle closely related subprob-\nlems. These algorithms typically follow the divide-and-conquer method: they\nbreak the problem into several subproblems that are similar to the original prob-\nlem but smaller in size, solve the subproblems recursively, and then combine these\nsolutions to create a solution to the original problem.\nIn the divide-and-conquer method, if the problem is small enough4the base\ncase4you just solve it directly without recursing. Otherwise4the recursive case\n4you perform three characteristic steps:\nDivide the problem into one or more subproblems that are smaller instances of the\nsame problem.\nConquer the subproblems by solving them recursively.\nCombine the subproblem solutions to form a solution to the original problem.\nThe merge sort algorithm closely follows the divide-and-conquer method. In\neach step, it sorts a subarray AŒp W r�, starting with the entire array AŒ1 W n� and\nrecursing down to smaller and smaller subarrays. Here is how merge sort operates:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"ttkTL4NuM3"}],"key":"GZCqBnNzm7"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.3.2 Analyzing divide-and-conquer algorithms","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"zKAwSN8FTL"}],"label":"sec-2-3-2","identifier":"sec-2-3-2","html_id":"sec-2-3-2","key":"YoAJuYIJVQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When an algorithm contains a recursive call, you can often describe its running time by a ","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"ka3DQKCyo5"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"recurrence equation","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"swEB9Kg8Jo"}],"key":"Oz9bvuSQgJ"},{"type":"text","value":" or ","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"UhDuWKFSmZ"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"recurrence","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"skrMl6BzWS"}],"key":"BdWOwYYFrq"},{"type":"text","value":", which describes the overall running time on a problem of size ","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"ZGleSScWxW"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"n","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>","key":"QaOrKw7dW4"},{"type":"text","value":" in terms of the running time of the same algorithm on smaller inputs. You can then use mathematical tools to solve the recurrence and provide bounds on the performance of the algorithm.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"ySVesFicJi"}],"key":"FQM2bYe4Je"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A recurrence for the running time of a divide-an